<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Kamakau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Samuel Mānaiakalani Kamakau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Mānaiakalani Kamakau was a Hawaiian historian and scholar whose writings, published in Hawaiian-language newspapers and later compiled into books, became an invaluable resource on the Hawaiian people, culture and language. Born at Mokulēia on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>, he studied at Lahainaluna Seminary under Sheldon Dibble from 1833 and in 1841 helped form the first Hawaiian historical society, with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> as its president. From 1866 to 1871 he wrote serial histories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>, the House of Kamehameha and Hawaiʻi, while serving as a judge and legislator. They appeared in English as Ruling Chiefs of Hawaiʻi (1961) and Ka Poʻe Kahiko (1964).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-kamakau.jpg" alt="Samuel Kamakau" width="330" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown; derivative work by PawełMM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_Kamakau.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kamakau was born on 29 October 1815 at Mokulēia, Waialua, on the North Shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>, and crossed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> to enter Lahainaluna Seminary in 1833. His teacher Sheldon Dibble told the students to collect and preserve knowledge of Hawaiian culture, language and people. Kamakau later recalled that the teachers wanted Hawaiians to read their own history as the people of Albion read of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and William, and that the king thought the history of all the islands should be preserved from first to last.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Royal Hawaiian Historical Society, formed in 1841, had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> as president, William Richards as vice-president, Dibble as secretary and Kamakau as treasurer, with members such as John Young, Timothy Haʻalilio and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-malo">David Malo</a>. It disbanded after the kingdom's capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaina">Lahaina</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> in 1845. Kamakau married S. Hainakolo and settled in her hometown of Kīpahulu; their daughter Kukelani Kaʻaʻapookalani was born in December 1862, after which the couple moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>. In 1860 he left Congregational Protestantism for Roman Catholicism.</p><p>He served as a district judge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wailuku">Wailuku</a> and represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> in the House of Representatives from 1851 to 1860 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> from 1870 to 1876. His histories of 1866 to 1871 appeared in the Hawaiian language newspapers Ke Au ʻOkoʻa and Ka Nūpepa Kūʻokoʻa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kamakau died at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> on 5 September 1876 and was buried in the Maʻemaʻe Chapel Cemetery in Nuʻuanu Valley. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-malo">David Malo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-papa-i-i">John Papa ʻĪʻī</a> he is counted among the leading historians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. The Hawaii State Library named its Hawaii and Pacific Section after him in 1994, a Hawaiian immersion school in Kāneʻohe took his name in 2000, the Samuel M. Kamakau Award goes to the Hawaiʻi Book of the Year, and the state legislature declared 29 October 2005 Samuel Manaiakalani Kamakau Day.</p><p>A revised edition of Ruling Chiefs of Hawaiʻi appeared in 1992. In 2022 Awaiaulu published Ke Kumu Aupuni: The Foundation of Hawaiian Nationhood, the articles he wrote for Ka Nūpepa Kūʻokoʻa between October 1866 and February 1868, with new English translations by Puakea Nogelmeier.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel Kamakau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To invade Kauaʻi he built a fleet of peleleu war canoes, some 800 according to the historian <b>Samuel Kamakau</b>, only for an epidemic to break the expedition in 1804.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of what is known comes from the Hawaiian scholars David Malo, <b>Samuel Kamakau</b> and John Papa ʻĪʻī, writing after the kapu ended in 1819, and from European visitors’ journals.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mokulēia, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.58,-158.18&amp;year=1854" aria-label="Mokulēia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="304 1366 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Samuel Kamakau"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M754 1636h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M754 1636h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.58,-158.18&amp;year=1854">What was here in 1854? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
